/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - At constant volume, heat raises internal energy only.
   - Let v be molar volume in m³ mol⁻¹, T temperature in K, α thermal expansion coefficient in K⁻¹, and κT isothermal compressibility in Pa⁻¹.
   - Cp and Cv are molar heat capacities in J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; α = (1/v)(∂v/∂T)P; κT = −(1/v)(∂v/∂P)T.
   - For an ideal gas α = 1/T and κT = 1/P.
   - For liquids α is small and κT is very small, so CP − CV is only about 1 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ for water, not R.
   - Define γ = Cp/Cv.
   - Heat capacities are not truly constant.
   - v is molar volume; the derivative is at fixed T.
   - Cv is molar heat capacity at constant volume.
   - For an ideal gas both second derivatives are zero, so CP and CV depend only on T.
*/
