/* book Worked example: Q: Liquid water at 300 K and 1 bar has v = 18.08 × 10⁻⁶ m³ mol⁻¹, α = 2.75 × 10⁻⁴ K⁻¹ and κT = 4.51 × 10⁻¹⁰ Pa⁻¹ (IAPWS values). Calculate CP − CV, and γ given CP = 75.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.
   1. Write the relation: CP − CV = T v α²/κT.
   2. Square α: (2.75 × 10⁻⁴)² = 7.5625 × 10⁻⁸ K⁻².
   3. Multiply T v α²: 300 × 18.08 × 10⁻⁶ × 7.5625 × 10⁻⁸ = 4.1019 × 10⁻¹⁰ m³ mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.
   4. Divide by κT: 4.1019 × 10⁻¹⁰/4.51 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 0.910 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ (Pa·m³ = J).
   5. CV = 75.31 − 0.91 = 74.40 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, so γ = 75.31/74.40 = 1.012.
   6. Check: IAPWS gives CV = 74.41 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ directly. For a liquid the two heat capacities differ by about 1 %, so either may be used in rough heat balances.
   Answer: CP − CV = 0.910 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ and γ = 1.012 for liquid water at 300 K. */
