/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The Jacobian of two functions u(x, y) and v(x, y) is the determinant of the 2×2 matrix of their first partial derivatives.
   - u and v are any two state functions; x and y are the two independent variables chosen to describe the state; the subscript is the variable held constant.
   - Swapping the two functions flips the sign, J(u, v) = −J(v, u), and a repeated function gives zero, J(u, u) = 0.
   - the held-constant variable y appears in both Jacobians.
   - That identity is the whole method.
   - Any target derivative is handled in four steps.
   - The cyclic relation drops out for free.
   - each factor is a ratio of two Jacobians in the same pair, and the three ratios cancel to (−1)³ = −1.
   - Maxwell relations are Jacobian ratios too.
   - Take the pressure dependence of enthalpy.
*/
