/* book Worked example 2: Q: Use the Jacobian method to find (∂T/∂P)S, the temperature rise per unit pressure in reversible adiabatic compression, in terms of measurable properties. Evaluate it for liquid water at 25 °C (V = 18.07 × 10⁻⁶ m³/mol, α = 2.573 × 10⁻⁴ K⁻¹, CP = 75.33 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹) pumped from 1 to 100 bar.
   1. Target as a ratio: (∂T/∂P)S = J(T, S)/J(P, S).
   2. Numerator in (T, P): J(T, S) = (∂T/∂T)P(∂S/∂P)T − (∂T/∂P)T(∂S/∂T)P = (1)(∂S/∂P)T − 0 = −(∂V/∂T)P (Maxwell).
   3. Denominator in (T, P): J(P, S) = (∂P/∂T)P(∂S/∂P)T − (∂P/∂P)T(∂S/∂T)P = 0 − (1)(CP/T) = −CP/T.
   4. Ratio: (∂T/∂P)S = T(∂V/∂T)P/CP = TVα/CP.
   5. Water: 298.15 × 18.07 × 10⁻⁶ × 2.573 × 10⁻⁴/75.33 = 1.840 × 10⁻⁸ K/Pa.
   6. Over 99 × 10⁵ Pa: ΔT = 1.840 × 10⁻⁸ × 99 × 10⁵ = 0.182 K. IAPWS steam tables give 0.187 K, so a feed pump hardly warms the water.
   Answer: (∂T/∂P)S = TVα/CP; for water 1.84 × 10⁻⁸ K/Pa, a rise of 0.182 K from 1 to 100 bar. */
